**Theorem 8.1 (all equality vectors, with the full common class retained).**
Fix $s\in[0,1]$. Consider every finite common instrument of Definition
2.3 whose acquired high cost attains the sharp original four-sign mean
at any one of the three original heights. In weak and threshold strengths
its original twelve-vector is the single vector
$(g_y(s))_{y,e,n}$. Its supported acquired high effect is $G_0(s)$.
In strong strengths set

$$
t=\sqrt{pq}>0,\qquad v=\sqrt{(1-p)(1-q)}>0,\qquad
\omega=ph_0^2+qb^2\in(0,1),\qquad
D_*(s)=\omega v-(1-\omega)t.
\tag{CA.45}
$$

If $D_*(s)\ne0$, the equality twelve-vector is again that singleton.
Its supported acquired high effect is again $G_0(s)$.
If $D_*(s)=0$, put

$$
\gamma=\frac{t}{v}=\frac{\omega}{1-\omega}>0,\qquad
Y=\operatorname{diag}\bigl(p-\gamma(1-p),\ q-\gamma(1-q),\ -\gamma\bigr),
\qquad \Delta=\Theta(Y)=\operatorname{diag}(0,\delta_1,\delta_2),
\tag{CA.46}
$$

where, writing $Y_D=p-\gamma(1-p)$ and
$Y_T=q-\gamma(1-q)$,

$$
\delta_1=h_1^2Y_D+a^2Y_T,\qquad
\delta_2=h_2^2Y_D-\gamma c^2,
\qquad
\theta_{\max}=\min\left\{\frac12,\frac1{2\gamma},
 \frac{m_1}{2|\delta_1|},\frac{m_2}{2|\delta_2|}\right\}>0.
\tag{CA.47}
$$

A fraction with $\delta_i=0$ in this minimum means $+\infty$; it
imposes no constraint. The complete equality resource effects on $V$
and original twelve-vectors are exactly

$$
G_\theta=G_0(s)+2\theta t\kappa X_{01},\qquad
\left(g_y(s)+4\theta t\kappa\,en\,
 \frac{\sqrt{y(64-y)}}{128}\right)_{y,e,n},
\qquad -\theta_{\max}\leq\theta\leq\theta_{\max}.
\tag{CA.48}
$$

Every exceptional-segment vector has an actual instrument in the original
class with at most twelve branches and dimensions at most three. Every
singleton also has an actual attainer within the twenty-four-branch bound.
Equality at
one original mean therefore implies equality at all three means. This
classifies the resource vectors of every such original equality design;
it does not assert uniqueness of their full instruments or conditioned
channels.

Proof of necessity. By Lemma 7.2 the actual high aggregate $H$ has
diagonal $(p,q,0)$ and there is no supported acquired-to-live high slack.
In the weak range $q=0$. A zero diagonal entry of a positive matrix kills
its entire row and column, by its two-by-two principal minors. Apply this
to $H_+$ and $H_-$: only the $DD$ entry survives, with total $k$.
Its supported resource effect is therefore $kD^\dagger D=G_0(s)$.
This includes both $s=0$ and the threshold.

In the strong range the same positivity argument kills the $S$ rows of
both high matrices. The aggregate low coefficient $L=I-H$ satisfies

$$
\operatorname{tr}(B_0L)=1-p-\alpha(1-q)-\beta=0,
\qquad B_0=\operatorname{diag}(1,-\alpha,-\beta).
\tag{CA.49}
$$

Each low record matrix belongs to $\mathcal C_2$ and has nonpositive
$B_0$ trace. Their sum has trace zero, so each has trace zero separately.
The exact exposed-face statement of Theorem 5.1 makes each $L_r$ real.
Consequently $H=I-L$ is real and its only possible off-diagonal entry is
$H_{DT}=h\in\mathbb R$.

Write the $DT$ block diagonals of $H_+$ as $(A,C)$ and its off-diagonal
entry as $z$. Those of $H_-$ are $(p-A,q-C)$ and $h-z$.
Positivity, the triangle inequality and Cauchy--Schwarz give

$$
2\operatorname{Re}z-h
 \leq |z|+|h-z|
 \leq\sqrt{AC}+\sqrt{(p-A)(q-C)}
 \leq\sqrt{pq}=t.
\tag{CA.50}
$$

For the low matrices write their plus $DT$ diagonal pair as $(A_L,C_L)$
and its off-diagonal entry as $w_L\in\mathbb R$. Their aggregate $DT$
entry is $-h$, so the full low chain is

$$
2w_L+h\leq |w_L|+|-h-w_L|
 \leq\sqrt{A_LC_L}
 +\sqrt{(1-p-A_L)(1-q-C_L)}\leq v.
\tag{CA.50a}
$$

The real $01$ binary effect, together with the zero
coordinate-$0$ failure effect, says
$\operatorname{Re}(H_++L_+)_{DT}=s/2$. Thus the two left sides in
these high and low bounds sum to $s=t+v$. Both bounds, and every
inequality within them, are equalities.

Equality in the high Cauchy--Schwarz inequality gives
$A/p=C/q=\tau\in[0,1]$. Equality in the preceding inequalities fixes
$z=\tau t$ and $h-z=-(1-\tau)t$, both real. Hence, with
$a_\pm=(\sqrt p,\ \pm\sqrt q,\ 0)^T$,

$$
H_+=\tau a_+a_+^T,\qquad
H_-=(1-\tau)a_-a_-^T.
\tag{CA.51}
$$

Equality in (CA.50a) gives
$A_L/(1-p)=C_L/(1-q)=\lambda\in[0,1]$,
$w_L=\lambda v$ and $-h-w_L=-(1-\lambda)v$.
Thus the low $DT$ blocks are
$\lambda b_+b_+^T$ and $(1-\lambda)b_-b_-^T$, where
$b_\pm=(\sqrt{1-p},\ \pm\sqrt{1-q})^T$.
Their separate trace-zero conditions (CA.49) give
$SS$ entries $\lambda$ and $1-\lambda$, because
$1-p-\alpha(1-q)=\beta>0$. In a positive block matrix, a vector in
the kernel of a principal block has zero coupling to the other block:
otherwise its quadratic form on that vector plus an arbitrarily small
multiple of the other vector would have a negative linear term.
Their $S$ coupling columns must therefore be $\xi b_+$ and
$\zeta b_-$, with $\xi,\zeta$ real. This also holds for a zero-weight
block, when positivity kills both its rows. The aggregate $DS$ and $TS$
entries vanish, giving $\xi+\zeta=\xi-\zeta=0$. Consequently

$$
L_+=\lambda B_+,\qquad L_-=(1-\lambda)B_-,\qquad
B_\pm=\begin{pmatrix}
1-p&\pm v&0\\ \pm v&1-q&0\\0&0&1
\end{pmatrix}.
\tag{CA.52}
$$

These arguments include $\tau$ or $\lambda$ equal to zero or one.
No strictly positive branch weight has been assumed.

Set $\epsilon=\tau-1/2$ and $\eta=\lambda-1/2$.
The aggregate coefficient sum and the coordinate-$0$ binary effect give,
respectively,

$$
\epsilon t+\eta v=0,\qquad
\epsilon\omega+\eta(1-\omega)=0.
\tag{CA.53}
$$

Here $\omega=ph_0^2+qb^2>0$, and
$1-\omega=(1-p)h_0^2+(1-q)b^2+d^2>0$ by normalization.
If $D_*\ne0$, this system has only $\epsilon=\eta=0$.
Equations (CA.51) then give aggregate $H=\operatorname{diag}(p,q,0)$
and resource effect $G_0(s)$.

If $D_*=0$, all solutions are
$\tau=1/2+\theta$, $\lambda=1/2-\gamma\theta$.
The coefficient matrices (CA.51)--(CA.52) are positive exactly when
$|\theta|\leq1/2$ and $|\gamma\theta|\leq1/2$.
Their plus total is $Z_+^0+\theta Y$: its $DT$ entry stays $s/2$,
and all other off-diagonal entries vanish. Its failure remainder is
therefore

$$
N_+=\frac M2-\theta\Delta,\qquad
N_-=\frac M2+\theta\Delta.
\tag{CA.54}
$$

The coordinate-$0$ entry of $\Delta$ is
$\omega-\gamma(1-\omega)=0$. The inequalities
$0\preceq N_+\preceq M$ are exactly
$|\theta\delta_i|\leq m_i/2$ for $i=1,2$.
They give precisely the remaining bounds in (CA.47), including the
zero-$\delta_i$ cases. All $m_i$ are strictly positive, so the interval
has positive length. Finally the high aggregate has $DT$ entry
$2\theta t$; applying $\Theta$ gives the first formula (CA.48).
The original pure states have real $01$ coherence
$en\sqrt{y(64-y)}/128$, proving its second formula.
Sufficiency, including endpoint attainment by genuine instruments, is
proved next. $\square$

